-- title:
--   Definitions for A Tight High-Order Entropic Quantum Uncertainty Relation With Applications
-- statement:
--   The objects the formalized statements of *A Tight High-Order Entropic Quantum Uncertainty Relation With Applications* (arXiv:quant-ph/0612014) are stated in. Each was checked against the paper by a blind read-back and a moderator.
--
--   - `massAt`: Section 2.2, p.6: pushforward subprobability mass, without renormalization.
--   - `retainedConditional`: Section 2.2, p.6: retained P[X=x,E | Y=y] uses the ORIGINAL marginal P[Y=y]. Unsupported y contribute zero. Alphabets may be arbitrary discrete types.
--   - `EntropyThreshold`: Section 2.2, p.6: an entropy threshold achieved by fractional event deletion. Any q≤p is P[w,E] for an event on an extension of the original space. The original p will always be normalized in theorem applications.
--   - `smoothMin`: Section 2.2, p.6: smooth worst-case classical conditional min-entropy in bits. EReal preserves +infinity when epsilon≥1, including the n=0 uncertainty bounds. For epsilon<0 there is no admissible deletion of a normalized law: value -infinity.
--   - `smoothJoint`: Section 2.2, p.6: unconditional event-smoothed entropy.
--   - `localBasisColumn`: Definition 3.2, p.7: columns of an indexed orthonormal measurement basis.
--   - `basisBornLaw`: Section 2.1, p.5: the Born law of a basis measurement on a density matrix.
--   - `AverageUncertainty`: Definition 3.2, p.7: average entropy bound for EVERY single-system density state.
--   - `tensorBasisColumn`: Theorem 3.3, p.7: tensor product of the independently selected local basis columns.
--   - `measurementExperiment`: Theorem 3.3, pp.7-8: joint P[X=x,Theta=theta]. No product-state assumption; the density matrix can be mixed and entangled. The uniform factor samples Theta independently of rho, with one fresh uniform basis for each subsystem.
--   - `accumulationError`: Theorem 3.1, p.6: its explicit smoothing error, with the square on the logarithm.
--   - `bb84Error`: Corollary 3.4, p.8: exactly the printed BB84 error.
--   - `bb84Basis`: Section 2.1, p.5: false is +, true is ×.
--   - `sixBasis`: Section 2.1, p.5: +, ×, and circular bases, in that order. The circular columns are (|0>+i|1>)/sqrt(2) and (|0>-i|1>)/sqrt(2).
--   - `history`: Theorem 3.1, p.6: all increments preceding i, as a finite history.
--   - `nextLaw`: Theorem 3.1, pp.6-7: Shannon law at a history, using the original history probability. The theorem imposes its entropy lower bound only at supported histories.
--   - `previousSigma`: Definition 2.2, p.6: sigma algebra generated by all preceding real increments.
--   - `MartingaleDifferences`: Definition 2.2, p.6: real measurable, integrable martingale differences, with conditional expectations zero almost surely. Measurability ensures that previousSigma is a sub-sigma-algebra, excluding condExp's junk-zero branch.
--   - `conditionalShannon`: Section 3, p.8: conditional Shannon entropy for a finite joint law.
--   - `IsNormalizedCQBlocks`: Section 2.1, p.5: a ccq-state represented by unnormalized PSD quantum blocks. The block traces give the joint classical distribution.
--   - `cqBlockOperator`: Section 2.1, p.5: the full block-diagonal density matrix of a cq-state.
--   - `TwoUniversal`: Section 4.1, p.8: a uniformly indexed nonempty two-universal family. Injectivity of hash identifies the indices with the actual set of functions.
--   - `hashedBlocks`: Theorem 4.1, p.8: apply an independent, uniformly sampled hash, keeping its seed and U. Quantum blocks incorporate the original ccq-state exactly.
--   - `uniformFirst`: Section 2.1 and Theorem 4.1, pp.5,8: replace the first classical register by uniform independent mass, preserving its entire remaining marginal.
--   - `entropyFactor`: Theorem 4.1, p.8: extended interpretation of 2^(-H/2). At H=+infinity the factor is zero; at H=-infinity it is +infinity.
--   - `selected`: Lemma 4.2, p.9: selected alphabet tagged by C; true selects X0, false selects X1. The tag also carries the binary C in the JOINT output.
--   - `fixedMin`: Appendix A.2, pp.18-19: fixed-reference UNSMOOTHED quantum min-entropy. No optimization over the reference sigma. EReal records infinite endpoints.
--   - `reorderedCQ`: Lemma A.2, pp.18-19: reordering X,U,Q to (X,Q),U places only U after the bar.
--   - `cqClassicalOperator`: Lemma A.2, pp.18-19: classical XU marginal obtained by tracing Q.
--   - `referenceMarginal`: Lemma A.2, pp.18-19: original fixed reference rho_U.
--   - `smoothFixedMin`: Lemma A.3, p.19, with its explicitly cited [28], Renner thesis, arXiv:quant-ph/0512258v2 Definition 3.2.1 (PDF pp.45-46): original fixed-reference quantum smoothing uses FULL trace-norm radius epsilon*Tr(rho) and PSD operators of trace at most Tr(rho). It is neither event deletion nor purified distance.
--   - `haarBound`: Proposition B.2, p.21: harmonic expression, with NATURAL ln(2).
--   - `haarMeasurementEntropy`: Proposition B.2, pp.20-21: entropy in the basis of the columns of U, with normalized Haar measure supplied by the audited Schur.unitaryHaar.
--   - `paddedRestriction`: Figure 1, p.10: compress the selected substring in increasing wire order, then pad its RIGHT end by zeros to length n. This is not coordinate masking.
--   - `otOutputs`: Figure 1, p.10: Alice's two outputs for the actual prepared x and theta.
--   - `honestOutcomeProb`: Figures 1 and 2, pp.10,12: honest measurement of all incoming qubits in the common basis selected by c.
--   - `HonestOTCorrect`: Figure 1, p.10: actual honest correctness as a zero failure probability, computed from preparation, measurement, substring padding, and hashing. There is no assumed correctness flag.
--   - `StorageMap`: Section 4.3, p.11: a completely general checkpoint map, with arbitrarily large finite classical storage Z and AT MOST q coherent qubits. Ancillas, measurements, and arbitrary computation before the checkpoint are absorbed into this channel. Classical Z has no coherent off-diagonal blocks.
--   - `OTTranscript`: Figure 1, p.10: public announcement theta,f0,f1, encoded by two hash indices.
--   - `ReceiverAttack`: Section 4.3, p.11: after revelation, quantum memory is UNRESTRICTED. The final channel may depend on the entire transcript and act on all classical storage and the q stored qubits. All output dimensions are quantified without a bound.
--   - `otSenderBlocks`: Figure 1, pp.10-11: exact final cq blocks for an arbitrary receiver. X and theta are uniform; hash seeds are fresh, uniform, independent of the attack. The transcript is retained in addition to the attacker's arbitrary final E.
--   - `BinaryExtension`: Section 2.1 and Definition 4.4, pp.5,9: a classical binary extension of a cq-state, retaining its exact original marginal (also at null outcomes).
--   - `senderDistance`: Definition 4.4, p.9: distance from uniform of S_(1-C), conditioned on S_C,C AND the full final view. The sum is exactly half trace norm for these block-diagonal classical registers. true means C=1 (secret S0).
--   - `OTSenderSecure`: Definition 4.4 and Figure 1, pp.9-11: full sender security, uniformly over all hash families and receiver attacks. Z and E have no a priori size bound; only the coherent checkpoint register has q qubits.
--   - `Negligible`: Definition 4.4, p.9: negligible nonnegative security error, smaller asymptotically than every inverse polynomial. Finite initial n are unrestricted.
--   - `OTLinearSlack`: Theorem 4.6 and p.11 improvement: POSITIVE LINEAR slack, with universal constant and threshold quantified before n. factor=2 is Theorem 4.6; factor=1 is the separately asserted improvement.
--   - `SenderAnnouncement`: Figure 1, p.10: a malicious sender may choose arbitrary announced bases and hash functions. These functions need not be sampled honestly.
--   - `SenderAttack`: Proposition 4.5, pp.10-11: arbitrary sent n-qubit state entangled with private memory M, followed by an arbitrary adaptive classical announcement and final quantum memory E. There is no receiver-to-sender message in Figure 1.
--   - `receiverResidual`: Proposition 4.5 proof, pp.10-11: residual unnormalized private state after the honest receiver measures the sent system in the common basis c.
--   - `receiverOutput`: Figure 1, p.10: Bob hashes his measured, compressed, zero-padded substring.
--   - `otReceiverBlocks`: Proposition 4.5, pp.10-11: actual C,Y,announcement,E blocks. C is sampled independently of the sender's initial state, with any prescribed law pc.
--   - `OTPerfectReceiverSecure`: Definition 4.4, p.9: exact receiver-security extraction/extension. The extension preserves C,Y and the sender's WHOLE view. Y=S'_C surely, and C is independent of (S'_0,S'_1,announcement,E). Unrestricted M,E model arbitrary finite private memories, with no computational or storage-size bound.
--   - `Opening`: Definition 5.1, p.12: arbitrary opening strategy, with access to classical Z and the residual quantum system I; output is an advertised bit and an opening message. A complete POVM includes every possible randomized quantum strategy.
--   - `IsBitKernel`: Definition 5.1, p.12: classical extension B' given V,Z only, preserving the conditional quantum blocks. The source's omitted sum over z is restored as required by the immediately preceding ccq expression.
--   - `bindingMass`: Definition 5.1, p.12: the original marginal probability P[B'=b].
--   - `openingSuccess`: Section 5, p.12: unconditional success probability of a specified opener on the fixed postcommit ccq-state.
--   - `oppositeSuccess`: Definition 5.1, p.12: kernel-weighted, still UNNORMALIZED, success probability on branch B'=branch when trying to open the opposite bit.
--   - `BindingKernel`: Definition 5.1, p.12: ONE kernel chosen before ALL opening strategies. Conditional success is required only on supported B' branches, retaining Z. The positivity guard excludes totalized division at a null branch.
--   - `StronglyBinding`: Definition 5.1, p.12: the full strong binding property on a postcommit state.
--   - `commitmentAccept`: Figure 2, p.12: the honest verifier checks EVERY matching-basis position.
--   - `commitmentBlocks`: Figure 2, p.12: actual postcommit state from uniform x,theta and the committer's arbitrary checkpoint channel. V=(x,theta) is fully classical; Z is all classical committer information, retained together with q quantum qubits.
--   - `HonestCommitCorrect`: Figure 2, p.12: honest prepare, measure in b, open b and the full outcome, then check all matching positions. Correctness is a computed failure probability.
-- source:
--   A Tight High-Order Entropic Quantum Uncertainty Relation With Applications, arXiv:quant-ph/0612014v2, https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/0612014v2

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Defs_Gates
import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Defs_Information
import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Defs_Channel
import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Defs_QuantumOutputMetrics
import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Defs_SchurUnitaryEnsembles
import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Information100_P27

/-!
Statements from Damgard, Fehr, Renner, Salvail and Schaffner,
arXiv:quant-ph/0612014v2. All page references are PDF pages.
Local definitions implement this paper's event deletion and fixed-reference conventions.
The classical entropy construction follows the audited event-threshold implementation in
Claimed50.P31, pp.5-6, without importing that statement module.
-/

noncomputable section

namespace QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Gap20.P03

open MeasureTheory Filter
open scoped ENNReal ComplexConjugate ComplexOrder Topology

-- Exact accepted finite Shannon entropy in bits (zero log zero is zero).
open QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Information100.P27 (shannon)

attribute [local instance] Classical.propDecidable

/-- Section 2.2, p.6: pushforward subprobability mass, without renormalization. -/
def massAt {W A : Type*} (p : W → ℝ≥0∞) (X : W → A) (a : A) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  ∑' w, if X w = a then p w else 0

/-- Section 2.2, p.6: retained P[X=x,E | Y=y] uses the ORIGINAL marginal P[Y=y].
Unsupported y contribute zero. Alphabets may be arbitrary discrete types. -/
def retainedConditional {W A B : Type*} (p q : W → ℝ≥0∞)
    (X : W → A) (Y : W → B) (x : A) (y : B) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  if massAt p Y y = 0 then 0 else
    massAt q (fun w => (X w, Y w)) (x,y) / massAt p Y y

/-- Section 2.2, p.6: an entropy threshold achieved by fractional event deletion.
Any q≤p is P[w,E] for an event on an extension of the original space.
The original p will always be normalized in theorem applications. -/
def EntropyThreshold {W A B : Type*} (p : W → ℝ≥0∞)
    (X : W → A) (Y : W → B) (epsilon r : ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∃ q : W → ℝ≥0∞, (∀ w, q w ≤ p w) ∧
    ENNReal.ofReal (1-epsilon) ≤ ∑' w, q w ∧
    ∀ x y, retainedConditional p q X Y x y ≤ ENNReal.ofReal (Real.rpow 2 (-r))

/-- Section 2.2, p.6: smooth worst-case classical conditional min-entropy in bits.
EReal preserves +infinity when epsilon≥1, including the n=0 uncertainty bounds.
For epsilon<0 there is no admissible deletion of a normalized law: value -infinity. -/
def smoothMin {W A B : Type*} (p : W → ℝ≥0∞)
    (X : W → A) (Y : W → B) (epsilon : ℝ) : EReal :=
  sSup {t | ∃ r : ℝ, t = (r : EReal) ∧ EntropyThreshold p X Y epsilon r}

/-- Section 2.2, p.6: unconditional event-smoothed entropy. -/
def smoothJoint {W A : Type*} (p : W → ℝ≥0∞) (X : W → A) (epsilon : ℝ) : EReal :=
  smoothMin p X (fun _ => ()) epsilon

/-- Definition 3.2, p.7: columns of an indexed orthonormal measurement basis. -/
def localBasisColumn {I : Type*} (B : Matrix I I ℂ) (x : I) : EuclideanSpace ℂ I :=
  WithLp.toLp 2 (fun z => B z x)

/-- Section 2.1, p.5: the Born law of a basis measurement on a density matrix. -/
def basisBornLaw {I : Type*} [Fintype I] (B rho : Matrix I I ℂ) (x : I) : ℝ :=
  effectProb (pureDensity (localBasisColumn B x)) rho

/-- Definition 3.2, p.7: average entropy bound for EVERY single-system density state. -/
def AverageUncertainty {I : Type} {B : Type*} [Fintype I] [Fintype B]
    (bases : B → Matrix I I ℂ) (h : ℝ) : Prop :=
  0 ≤ h ∧ ∀ rho : Matrix I I ℂ, IsDensityMatrix rho →
    h ≤ (Fintype.card B : ℝ)⁻¹ * ∑ b, shannon (basisBornLaw (bases b) rho)

/-- Theorem 3.3, p.7: tensor product of the independently selected local basis columns. -/
def tensorBasisColumn {I B : Type*} {n : ℕ} (bases : B → Matrix I I ℂ)
    (theta : Fin n → B) (x : Fin n → I) : EuclideanSpace ℂ (Fin n → I) :=
  WithLp.toLp 2 (fun z => ∏ i, bases (theta i) (z i) (x i))

/-- Theorem 3.3, pp.7-8: joint P[X=x,Theta=theta]. No product-state assumption;
the density matrix can be mixed and entangled. The uniform factor samples Theta
independently of rho, with one fresh uniform basis for each subsystem. -/
def measurementExperiment {I B : Type*} [Fintype I] [Fintype B] {n : ℕ}
    (bases : B → Matrix I I ℂ) (rho : Matrix (Fin n → I) (Fin n → I) ℂ) :
    ((Fin n → I) × (Fin n → B)) → ℝ≥0∞ :=
  fun w => ENNReal.ofReal ((Fintype.card B : ℝ) ^ n)⁻¹ *
    ENNReal.ofReal (effectProb (pureDensity (tensorBasisColumn bases w.2 w.1)) rho)

/-- Theorem 3.1, p.6: its explicit smoothing error, with the square on the logarithm. -/
def accumulationError (alphabetSize n : ℕ) (lambda : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  Real.exp (-(lambda^2 * n) / (32 * (Real.logb 2 (alphabetSize / lambda))^2))

/-- Corollary 3.4, p.8: exactly the printed BB84 error. -/
def bb84Error (n : ℕ) (lambda : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  Real.exp (-(lambda^2 * n) / (32 * (2 - Real.logb 2 lambda)^2))

/-- Section 2.1, p.5: false is +, true is ×. -/
def bb84Basis (b : Bool) : Matrix Bool Bool ℂ := if b then hadamard else 1

/-- Section 2.1, p.5: +, ×, and circular bases, in that order. The circular
columns are (|0>+i|1>)/sqrt(2) and (|0>-i|1>)/sqrt(2). -/
def sixBasis (b : Fin 3) : Matrix Bool Bool ℂ :=
  if b = 0 then 1 else if b = 1 then hadamard else phaseS * hadamard

/-- Theorem 3.1, p.6: all increments preceding i, as a finite history. -/
def history {A : Type*} {n : ℕ} (i : Fin n) (z : Fin n → A) : Fin i.val → A :=
  fun j => z ⟨j.val, Nat.lt_trans j.isLt i.isLt⟩

/-- Theorem 3.1, pp.6-7: Shannon law at a history, using the original history
probability. The theorem imposes its entropy lower bound only at supported histories. -/
def nextLaw {A : Type*} [Fintype A] {n : ℕ} (p : PMF (Fin n → A))
    (i : Fin n) (v : Fin i.val → A) (a : A) : ℝ :=
  (retainedConditional (fun z => p z) (fun z => p z)
    (fun z => z i) (history i) a v).toReal

/-- Definition 2.2, p.6: sigma algebra generated by all preceding real increments. -/
def previousSigma {W : Type*} {n : ℕ} (R : Fin n → W → ℝ) (i : Fin n) :
    MeasurableSpace W :=
  MeasurableSpace.comap (fun w => fun j : Fin i.val =>
    R ⟨j.val, Nat.lt_trans j.isLt i.isLt⟩ w) inferInstance

/-- Definition 2.2, p.6: real measurable, integrable martingale differences,
with conditional expectations zero almost surely. Measurability ensures that
previousSigma is a sub-sigma-algebra, excluding condExp's junk-zero branch. -/
def MartingaleDifferences {W : Type*} [mW : MeasurableSpace W] {n : ℕ}
    (mu : Measure W) (R : Fin n → W → ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ i, Measurable (R i) ∧ Integrable (R i) mu ∧ previousSigma R i ≤ mW ∧
    (condExp (previousSigma R i) mu (R i) =ᵐ[mu] fun _ => (0 : ℝ))

/-- Section 3, p.8: conditional Shannon entropy for a finite joint law. -/
def conditionalShannon {A B : Type} [Fintype A] [Fintype B]
    (p : (A × B) → ℝ≥0∞) : ℝ :=
  ∑ b, (massAt p Prod.snd b).toReal *
    shannon (fun a => (retainedConditional p p Prod.fst Prod.snd a b).toReal)

/-- Section 2.1, p.5: a ccq-state represented by unnormalized PSD quantum blocks.
The block traces give the joint classical distribution. -/
def IsNormalizedCQBlocks {A I : Type*} [Fintype A] [Fintype I]
    (blocks : A → Matrix I I ℂ) : Prop :=
  (∀ a, (blocks a).PosSemidef) ∧ ∑ a, (blocks a).trace = 1

/-- Section 2.1, p.5: the full block-diagonal density matrix of a cq-state. -/
def cqBlockOperator {A I : Type*} (blocks : A → Matrix I I ℂ) :
    Matrix (A × I) (A × I) ℂ :=
  fun v w => if v.1 = w.1 then blocks v.1 v.2 w.2 else 0

/-- Section 4.1, p.8: a uniformly indexed nonempty two-universal family.
Injectivity of hash identifies the indices with the actual set of functions. -/
def TwoUniversal {K : Type*} [Fintype K] {n ell : ℕ}
    (hash : K → Qubits n → Qubits ell) : Prop :=
  Function.Injective hash ∧ ∀ x y, x ≠ y →
    (∑ k, if hash k x = hash k y then (1:ℝ) else 0) / Fintype.card K ≤
      ((2:ℝ)^ell)⁻¹

/-- Theorem 4.1, p.8: apply an independent, uniformly sampled hash, keeping
its seed and U. Quantum blocks incorporate the original ccq-state exactly. -/
def hashedBlocks {K U I : Type*} [Fintype K] {n ell : ℕ}
    (hash : K → Qubits n → Qubits ell) (blocks : (Qubits n × U) → Matrix I I ℂ) :
    (Qubits ell × (K × U)) → Matrix I I ℂ :=
  fun a => ((Fintype.card K : ℂ)⁻¹) •
    ∑ x, if hash a.2.1 x = a.1 then blocks (x,a.2.2) else 0

/-- Section 2.1 and Theorem 4.1, pp.5,8: replace the first classical register
by uniform independent mass, preserving its entire remaining marginal. -/
def uniformFirst {S T I : Type*} [Fintype S]
    (blocks : (S × T) → Matrix I I ℂ) : (S × T) → Matrix I I ℂ :=
  fun a => ((Fintype.card S : ℂ)⁻¹) • ∑ s, blocks (s,a.2)

/-- Theorem 4.1, p.8: extended interpretation of 2^(-H/2).
At H=+infinity the factor is zero; at H=-infinity it is +infinity. -/
def entropyFactor (H : EReal) : ℝ≥0∞ :=
  if H = ⊤ then 0 else if H = ⊥ then ⊤ else
    ENNReal.ofReal (Real.rpow 2 (-H.toReal/2))

/-- Lemma 4.2, p.9: selected alphabet tagged by C; true selects X0,
false selects X1. The tag also carries the binary C in the JOINT output. -/
def selected {A0 A1 : Type*} (x0 : A0) (x1 : A1) (c : Bool) : Sum A0 A1 :=
  if c then Sum.inl x0 else Sum.inr x1

/-- Appendix A.2, pp.18-19: fixed-reference UNSMOOTHED quantum min-entropy.
No optimization over the reference sigma. EReal records infinite endpoints. -/
def fixedMin {A B : Type*} [Fintype A] [DecidableEq A] [Fintype B]
    (rho : Matrix (A × B) (A × B) ℂ) (sigma : Matrix B B ℂ) : EReal :=
  sSup {t | ∃ r : ℝ, t = (r:EReal) ∧
    (((Real.rpow 2 (-r):ℝ):ℂ) • Matrix.kronecker (1:Matrix A A ℂ) sigma - rho).PosSemidef}

/-- Lemma A.2, pp.18-19: reordering X,U,Q to (X,Q),U places only U after the bar. -/
def reorderedCQ {X U Q : Type*} (blocks : (X × U) → Matrix Q Q ℂ) :
    Matrix ((X × Q) × U) ((X × Q) × U) ℂ :=
  fun a b => if a.1.1 = b.1.1 ∧ a.2 = b.2 then blocks (a.1.1,a.2) a.1.2 b.1.2 else 0

/-- Lemma A.2, pp.18-19: classical XU marginal obtained by tracing Q. -/
def cqClassicalOperator {X U Q : Type*} [Fintype Q]
    (blocks : (X × U) → Matrix Q Q ℂ) : Matrix (X × U) (X × U) ℂ :=
  Matrix.diagonal (fun a => (blocks a).trace)

/-- Lemma A.2, pp.18-19: original fixed reference rho_U. -/
def referenceMarginal {X U Q : Type*} [Fintype X] [Fintype Q]
    (blocks : (X × U) → Matrix Q Q ℂ) : Matrix U U ℂ :=
  Matrix.diagonal (fun u => ∑ x, (blocks (x,u)).trace)

/-- Lemma A.3, p.19, with its explicitly cited [28], Renner thesis,
arXiv:quant-ph/0512258v2 Definition 3.2.1 (PDF pp.45-46): original fixed-reference
quantum smoothing uses FULL trace-norm radius epsilon*Tr(rho) and PSD operators
of trace at most Tr(rho). It is neither event deletion nor purified distance. -/
def smoothFixedMin {A B : Type*} [Fintype A] [DecidableEq A] [Fintype B] [DecidableEq B]
    (rho : Matrix (A × B) (A × B) ℂ) (sigma : Matrix B B ℂ) (epsilon : ℝ) : EReal :=
  sSup {t | ∃ tau : Matrix (A × B) (A × B) ℂ,
    tau.PosSemidef ∧ tau.trace.re ≤ rho.trace.re ∧
    traceNorm (tau-rho) ≤ rho.trace.re * epsilon ∧ t = fixedMin tau sigma}

/-- Zero radius retains the original operator as an admissible point of its
historical smoothing ball (Lemma A.3, p.19, cited [28] Definition 3.2.1). -/
example {A B : Type*} [Fintype A] [DecidableEq A] [Fintype B] [DecidableEq B]
    (rho : Matrix (A × B) (A × B) ℂ) (hrho : rho.PosSemidef) :
    rho.PosSemidef ∧ rho.trace.re ≤ rho.trace.re ∧ traceNorm (rho-rho) ≤ rho.trace.re*0 := by
  simp [hrho, traceNorm, cfc_zero]

/-- Proposition B.2, p.21: harmonic expression, with NATURAL ln(2). -/
def haarBound (d : ℕ) : ℝ :=
  (∑ i ∈ Finset.Icc 2 d, (i:ℝ)⁻¹) / Real.log 2

/-- Proposition B.2, pp.20-21: entropy in the basis of the columns of U,
with normalized Haar measure supplied by the audited Schur.unitaryHaar. -/
def haarMeasurementEntropy (d : ℕ) (rho : Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) ℂ)
    (U : Matrix.unitaryGroup (Fin d) ℂ) : ℝ :=
  shannon (basisBornLaw (U:Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) ℂ) rho)

/-- Empty tensor products have unit amplitude (Theorem 3.3, p.7, n=0). -/
example {I B : Type*} (bases : B → Matrix I I ℂ)
    (theta : Fin 0 → B) (x z : Fin 0 → I) : tensorBasisColumn bases theta x z = 1 := by
  simp [tensorBasisColumn]

/-- Smoothing error is one at n=0 (Corollary 3.4, p.8). -/
example (lambda : ℝ) : bb84Error 0 lambda = 1 := by simp [bb84Error]

/-- With epsilon=1 the zero retained mass attains every finite threshold (p.6). -/
example {W A B : Type*} (p : W → ℝ≥0∞) (X : W → A) (Y : W → B) (r : ℝ) :
    EntropyThreshold p X Y 1 r := by
  refine ⟨fun _ => 0, fun _ => bot_le, by simp, ?_⟩
  intro x y
  simp [retainedConditional, massAt]

/-- The circular |1> column has -i/sqrt(2), fixing the relative sign (p.5). -/
example : sixBasis 2 true true = -Complex.I * ((Real.sqrt 2)⁻¹:ℂ) := by
  simp [sixBasis, phaseS, hadamard, Matrix.mul_apply, Fintype.sum_bool]

/-- No quantum conditional entropy is substituted for the classical entropy (p.6). -/
example {W A B : Type*} (p q : W → ℝ≥0∞) (X : W → A) (Y : W → B)
    (x : A) (y : B) (hy : massAt p Y y = 0) : retainedConditional p q X Y x y = 0 := by
  simp [retainedConditional, hy]

/-- Selected alphabets are tagged; false selects X1 (Lemma 4.2, p.9). -/
example {A0 A1 : Type*} (x0 : A0) (x1 : A1) : selected x0 x1 false = Sum.inr x1 := by simp [selected]

/-- The all-deleted entropy contributes zero in privacy amplification (p.8). -/
example : entropyFactor ⊤ = 0 := by simp [entropyFactor]

/-- A one-dimensional system has harmonic uncertainty zero (Proposition B.2, p.21). -/
example : haarBound 1 = 0 := by simp [haarBound]

/-- Figure 1, p.10: compress the selected substring in increasing wire order,
then pad its RIGHT end by zeros to length n. This is not coordinate masking. -/
def paddedRestriction {n : ℕ} (x theta : Qubits n) (b : Bool) : Qubits n :=
  fun j => (((List.finRange n).filter (fun i => theta i == b)).map x).getD j.val false

/-- Figure 1, p.10: Alice's two outputs for the actual prepared x and theta. -/
def otOutputs {n ell : ℕ} {K : Type*} (hash : K → Qubits n → Qubits ell)
    (x theta : Qubits n) (k0 k1 : K) : Qubits ell × Qubits ell :=
  (hash k0 (paddedRestriction x theta false), hash k1 (paddedRestriction x theta true))

/-- Figures 1 and 2, pp.10,12: honest measurement of all incoming qubits
in the common basis selected by c. -/
def honestOutcomeProb {n : ℕ} (x theta : Qubits n) (c : Bool) (y : Qubits n) : ℝ :=
  effectProb (pureDensity (tensorBasisColumn bb84Basis (fun _ => c) y))
    (pureDensity (tensorBasisColumn bb84Basis theta x))

/-- Figure 1, p.10: actual honest correctness as a zero failure probability,
computed from preparation, measurement, substring padding, and hashing.
There is no assumed correctness flag. -/
def HonestOTCorrect (n ell : ℕ) : Prop :=
  ∀ (f0 f1 : Qubits n → Qubits ell) (c : Bool),
    ∑ x : Qubits n, ∑ theta : Qubits n, ∑ y : Qubits n,
      ((4:ℝ)^n)⁻¹ * honestOutcomeProb x theta c y *
        (if (if c then f1 else f0) (paddedRestriction y theta c) =
          (if c then f1 else f0) (paddedRestriction x theta c) then 0 else 1) = 0

/-- Section 4.3, p.11: a completely general checkpoint map, with arbitrarily
large finite classical storage Z and AT MOST q coherent qubits. Ancillas,
measurements, and arbitrary computation before the checkpoint are absorbed into
this channel. Classical Z has no coherent off-diagonal blocks. -/
structure StorageMap (n q : ℕ) (Z : Type*) [Fintype Z] [DecidableEq Z] where
  channel : Superop (Qubits n) (Z × Qubits q)
  physical : IsChannel channel
  classical : ∀ rho z z', z ≠ z' → ∀ a b,
    channel rho (z,a) (z',b) = 0

/-- Figure 1, p.10: public announcement theta,f0,f1, encoded by two hash indices. -/
abbrev OTTranscript (n : ℕ) (K : Type*) := Qubits n × (K × K)

/-- Section 4.3, p.11: after revelation, quantum memory is UNRESTRICTED.
The final channel may depend on the entire transcript and act on all classical
storage and the q stored qubits. All output dimensions are quantified without a bound. -/
structure ReceiverAttack (n q : ℕ) (K Z E : Type*)
    [Fintype Z] [DecidableEq Z] [Fintype E] [DecidableEq E] where
  storage : StorageMap n q Z
  finish : OTTranscript n K → Superop (Z × Qubits q) E
  physicalFinish : ∀ t, IsChannel (finish t)

/-- Figure 1, pp.10-11: exact final cq blocks for an arbitrary receiver.
X and theta are uniform; hash seeds are fresh, uniform, independent of the attack.
The transcript is retained in addition to the attacker's arbitrary final E. -/
def otSenderBlocks {n ell q : ℕ} {K Z E : Type*} [Fintype K]
    [Fintype Z] [DecidableEq Z] [Fintype E] [DecidableEq E]
    (hash : K → Qubits n → Qubits ell) (A : ReceiverAttack n q K Z E) :
    ((Qubits ell × Qubits ell) × OTTranscript n K) → Matrix E E ℂ :=
  fun a => (((4:ℂ)^n * (Fintype.card K:ℂ)^2)⁻¹) •
    ∑ x : Qubits n,
      if otOutputs hash x a.2.1 a.2.2.1 a.2.2.2 = a.1 then
        A.finish a.2 (A.storage.channel (pureDensity (tensorBasisColumn bb84Basis a.2.1 x)))
      else 0

/-- Section 2.1 and Definition 4.4, pp.5,9: a classical binary extension
of a cq-state, retaining its exact original marginal (also at null outcomes). -/
def BinaryExtension {A I : Type*} [Fintype I]
    (blocks : A → Matrix I I ℂ) (ext : Bool → A → Matrix I I ℂ) : Prop :=
  (∀ c a, (ext c a).PosSemidef) ∧ ∀ a, ext false a + ext true a = blocks a

/-- Definition 4.4, p.9: distance from uniform of S_(1-C), conditioned on
S_C,C AND the full final view. The sum is exactly half trace norm for these
block-diagonal classical registers. true means C=1 (secret S0). -/
def senderDistance {S T I : Type*} [Fintype S] [Fintype T] [Fintype I] [DecidableEq I]
    (ext : Bool → ((S × S) × T) → Matrix I I ℂ) : ℝ :=
  ∑ c : Bool, ∑ s0 : S, ∑ s1 : S, ∑ t : T,
    traceDistance (ext c ((s0,s1),t))
      (((Fintype.card S:ℂ)⁻¹) •
        ∑ secret : S, ext c ((if c then (secret,s1) else (s0,secret)),t))

/-- Definition 4.4 and Figure 1, pp.9-11: full sender security, uniformly
over all hash families and receiver attacks. Z and E have no a priori size bound;
only the coherent checkpoint register has q qubits. -/
def OTSenderSecure (n ell q : ℕ) (epsilon : ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ (K : Type) [Fintype K] [Nonempty K]
    (hash : K → Qubits n → Qubits ell), TwoUniversal hash →
    ∀ (Z E : Type) [Fintype Z] [DecidableEq Z] [Nonempty Z]
      [Fintype E] [DecidableEq E] [Nonempty E] (A : ReceiverAttack n q K Z E),
      ∃ ext : Bool → ((Qubits ell × Qubits ell) × OTTranscript n K) → Matrix E E ℂ,
        BinaryExtension (otSenderBlocks hash A) ext ∧ senderDistance ext ≤ epsilon

/-- Definition 4.4, p.9: negligible nonnegative security error, smaller
asymptotically than every inverse polynomial. Finite initial n are unrestricted. -/
def Negligible (epsilon : ℕ → ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∀ n, 0 ≤ epsilon n) ∧ ∀ k : ℕ,
    Tendsto (fun n : ℕ => (n:ℝ)^k * epsilon n) atTop (nhds 0)

/-- Theorem 4.6 and p.11 improvement: POSITIVE LINEAR slack, with universal
constant and threshold quantified before n. factor=2 is Theorem 4.6; factor=1
is the separately asserted improvement. -/
def OTLinearSlack (factor : ℝ) (ell q : ℕ → ℕ) : Prop :=
  ∃ a : ℝ, 0 < a ∧ ∃ N : ℕ, ∀ n ≥ N,
    a*n ≤ (n:ℝ)/4 - factor*ell n - q n

/-- Figure 1, p.10: a malicious sender may choose arbitrary announced bases
and hash functions. These functions need not be sampled honestly. -/
abbrev SenderAnnouncement (n ell : ℕ) :=
  Qubits n × ((Qubits n → Qubits ell) × (Qubits n → Qubits ell))

/-- Proposition 4.5, pp.10-11: arbitrary sent n-qubit state entangled with
private memory M, followed by an arbitrary adaptive classical announcement and
final quantum memory E. There is no receiver-to-sender message in Figure 1. -/
structure SenderAttack (n ell : ℕ) (M E : Type*)
    [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M] [Fintype E] [DecidableEq E] where
  initial : Matrix (Qubits n × M) (Qubits n × M) ℂ
  state : IsDensityMatrix initial
  announce : Superop M (SenderAnnouncement n ell × E)
  physical : IsChannel announce
  classical : ∀ rho t t', t ≠ t' → ∀ a b, announce rho (t,a) (t',b) = 0

/-- Proposition 4.5 proof, pp.10-11: residual unnormalized private state
after the honest receiver measures the sent system in the common basis c. -/
def receiverResidual {n : ℕ} {M : Type*}
    (rho : Matrix (Qubits n × M) (Qubits n × M) ℂ) (c : Bool) (y : Qubits n) :
    Matrix M M ℂ :=
  fun m m' => ∑ a : Qubits n, ∑ b : Qubits n,
    conj (tensorBasisColumn bb84Basis (fun _ => c) y a) * rho (a,m) (b,m') *
      tensorBasisColumn bb84Basis (fun _ => c) y b

/-- Figure 1, p.10: Bob hashes his measured, compressed, zero-padded substring. -/
def receiverOutput {n ell : ℕ} (t : SenderAnnouncement n ell) (c : Bool) (y : Qubits n) :
    Qubits ell := (if c then t.2.2 else t.2.1) (paddedRestriction y t.1 c)

/-- Proposition 4.5, pp.10-11: actual C,Y,announcement,E blocks. C is sampled
independently of the sender's initial state, with any prescribed law pc. -/
def otReceiverBlocks {n ell : ℕ} {M E : Type*}
    [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M] [Fintype E] [DecidableEq E]
    (A : SenderAttack n ell M E) (pc : PMF Bool) :
    (Bool × (Qubits ell × SenderAnnouncement n ell)) → Matrix E E ℂ :=
  fun w => ((pc w.1).toReal:ℂ) •
    ∑ y : Qubits n, if receiverOutput w.2.2 w.1 y = w.2.1 then
      (fun a b => A.announce (receiverResidual A.initial w.1 y) (w.2.2,a) (w.2.2,b)) else 0

/-- Definition 4.4, p.9: exact receiver-security extraction/extension.
The extension preserves C,Y and the sender's WHOLE view. Y=S'_C surely, and
C is independent of (S'_0,S'_1,announcement,E). Unrestricted M,E model arbitrary
finite private memories, with no computational or storage-size bound. -/
def OTPerfectReceiverSecure (n ell : ℕ) : Prop :=
  ∀ (M E : Type) [Fintype M] [DecidableEq M] [Nonempty M]
    [Fintype E] [DecidableEq E] [Nonempty E]
    (A : SenderAttack n ell M E) (pc : PMF Bool),
    ∃ ext : Bool → Qubits ell → (Qubits ell × Qubits ell) →
        SenderAnnouncement n ell → Matrix E E ℂ,
      (∀ c y s t, (ext c y s t).PosSemidef) ∧
      (∀ c y t, ∑ s, ext c y s t = otReceiverBlocks A pc (c,(y,t))) ∧
      (∀ c y s t, y ≠ (if c then s.2 else s.1) → ext c y s t = 0) ∧
      (∀ c s t, ∑ y, ext c y s t = ((pc c).toReal:ℂ) •
        ∑ c' : Bool, ∑ y, ext c' y s t)

/-- Definition 5.1, p.12: arbitrary opening strategy, with access to classical Z
and the residual quantum system I; output is an advertised bit and an opening
message. A complete POVM includes every possible randomized quantum strategy. -/
structure Opening (Z Message I : Type*) [Fintype Message] [Fintype I] [DecidableEq I] where
  effect : Z → Bool → Message → Matrix I I ℂ
  positive : ∀ z b m, (effect z b m).PosSemidef
  complete : ∀ z, ∑ b : Bool, ∑ m, effect z b m = 1

/-- Definition 5.1, p.12: classical extension B' given V,Z only, preserving
the conditional quantum blocks. The source's omitted sum over z is restored
as required by the immediately preceding ccq expression. -/
def IsBitKernel {V Z : Type*} (kernel : V → Z → Bool → ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∀ v z b, 0 ≤ kernel v z b) ∧ ∀ v z, kernel v z false + kernel v z true = 1

/-- Definition 5.1, p.12: the original marginal probability P[B'=b]. -/
def bindingMass {V Z I : Type*} [Fintype V] [Fintype Z] [Fintype I]
    (blocks : (V × Z) → Matrix I I ℂ) (kernel : V → Z → Bool → ℝ) (b : Bool) : ℝ :=
  ∑ v, ∑ z, kernel v z b * (blocks (v,z)).trace.re

/-- Section 5, p.12: unconditional success probability of a specified opener
on the fixed postcommit ccq-state. -/
def openingSuccess {V Z Message I : Type*} [Fintype V] [Fintype Z] [Fintype Message]
    [Fintype I] [DecidableEq I] (blocks : (V × Z) → Matrix I I ℂ)
    (accept : V → Bool → Message → Bool) (O : Opening Z Message I) (b : Bool) : ℝ :=
  ∑ v, ∑ z, ∑ m, if accept v b m then effectProb (O.effect z b m) (blocks (v,z)) else 0

/-- Definition 5.1, p.12: kernel-weighted, still UNNORMALIZED, success
probability on branch B'=branch when trying to open the opposite bit. -/
def oppositeSuccess {V Z Message I : Type*} [Fintype V] [Fintype Z] [Fintype Message]
    [Fintype I] [DecidableEq I] (blocks : (V × Z) → Matrix I I ℂ)
    (accept : V → Bool → Message → Bool) (kernel : V → Z → Bool → ℝ)
    (O : Opening Z Message I) (branch : Bool) : ℝ :=
  ∑ v, ∑ z, kernel v z branch *
    ∑ m, if accept v (!branch) m then effectProb (O.effect z (!branch) m) (blocks (v,z)) else 0

/-- Definition 5.1, p.12: ONE kernel chosen before ALL opening strategies.
Conditional success is required only on supported B' branches, retaining Z.
The positivity guard excludes totalized division at a null branch. -/
def BindingKernel {V Z Message I : Type*} [Fintype V] [Fintype Z] [Fintype Message]
    [Fintype I] [DecidableEq I] (blocks : (V × Z) → Matrix I I ℂ)
    (accept : V → Bool → Message → Bool) (epsilon : ℝ)
    (kernel : V → Z → Bool → ℝ) : Prop :=
  IsBitKernel kernel ∧ ∀ b, 0 < bindingMass blocks kernel b →
    ∀ O : Opening Z Message I,
      oppositeSuccess blocks accept kernel O b / bindingMass blocks kernel b ≤ epsilon

/-- Definition 5.1, p.12: the full strong binding property on a postcommit state. -/
def StronglyBinding {V Z Message I : Type*} [Fintype V] [Fintype Z] [Fintype Message]
    [Fintype I] [DecidableEq I] (blocks : (V × Z) → Matrix I I ℂ)
    (accept : V → Bool → Message → Bool) (epsilon : ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∃ kernel : V → Z → Bool → ℝ, BindingKernel blocks accept epsilon kernel

/-- Figure 2, p.12: the honest verifier checks EVERY matching-basis position. -/
def commitmentAccept {n : ℕ} (v : Qubits n × Qubits n) (b : Bool) (message : Qubits n) : Bool :=
  decide (∀ i : Fin n, v.2 i = b → v.1 i = message i)

/-- Figure 2, p.12: actual postcommit state from uniform x,theta and the
committer's arbitrary checkpoint channel. V=(x,theta) is fully classical; Z is
all classical committer information, retained together with q quantum qubits. -/
def commitmentBlocks {n q : ℕ} {Z : Type*} [Fintype Z] [DecidableEq Z]
    (A : StorageMap n q Z) : ((Qubits n × Qubits n) × Z) → Matrix (Qubits q) (Qubits q) ℂ :=
  fun a => (((4:ℂ)^n)⁻¹) •
    (fun i j => A.channel (pureDensity (tensorBasisColumn bb84Basis a.1.2 a.1.1)) (a.2,i) (a.2,j))

/-- Figure 2, p.12: honest prepare, measure in b, open b and the full outcome,
then check all matching positions. Correctness is a computed failure probability. -/
def HonestCommitCorrect (n : ℕ) : Prop :=
  ∀ b : Bool, ∑ x : Qubits n, ∑ theta : Qubits n, ∑ y : Qubits n,
    ((4:ℝ)^n)⁻¹ * honestOutcomeProb x theta b y *
      (if commitmentAccept (x,theta) b y then 0 else 1) = 0

/-- Compression removes the unselected position before padding (Figure 1, p.10). -/
example : paddedRestriction (![false,true]:Qubits 2) ![true,false] false 0 = true := rfl

/-- Empty commitments accept any opening string, as Figure 2's check requires. -/
example (b : Bool) (x theta y : Qubits 0) : commitmentAccept (x,theta) b y = true := by
  simp [commitmentAccept]

/-- Binary extension preserves every original block, including the transcript (p.9). -/
example {A I : Type*} [Fintype I] (blocks : A → Matrix I I ℂ)
    (ext : Bool → A → Matrix I I ℂ) (h : BinaryExtension blocks ext) (a : A) :
    ext false a + ext true a = blocks a := h.2 a

/-- At epsilon=0 the strong-binding test still uses the full opposing opener (p.12). -/
example {V Z Message I : Type*} [Fintype V] [Fintype Z] [Fintype Message]
    [Fintype I] [DecidableEq I] (blocks : (V × Z) → Matrix I I ℂ)
    (accept : V → Bool → Message → Bool) (kernel : V → Z → Bool → ℝ)
    (h : BindingKernel blocks accept 0 kernel) : IsBitKernel kernel := h.1

/-- Section 2.2, p.6: retaining half of a point mass leaves conditional mass
one half, since conditioning keeps the ORIGINAL denominator rather than normalizing q. -/
example : retainedConditional (fun _ : Unit => (1 : ℝ≥0∞))
    (fun _ : Unit => (1 / 2 : ℝ≥0∞)) (fun _ => ()) (fun _ => ()) () () = 1 / 2 := by
  simp [retainedConditional, massAt]

/-- Section 2.2, pp.5-6: the entropy of a deterministic singleton law is zero. -/
example : shannon (fun _ : Unit => (1 : ℝ)) = 0 := by
  simp [shannon]

/-- Definition 5.1, p.12: a fair bit is a normalized classical kernel, even
when the verifier and committer records have arbitrary alphabets. -/
example {V Z : Type*} : IsBitKernel (fun (_ : V) (_ : Z) (_ : Bool) => (1 / 2 : ℝ)) := by
  constructor
  · intro v z b
    norm_num
  · intro v z
    norm_num

end QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Gap20.P03


